AB  - Splenic torsion is an uncommon condition becoming clinically apparent when the spleen twists or rotates around the organ's vascular pedicle. In the case of a wandering spleen the organ is only attached to an elongated vascular pedicle while the peritoneal attachments are absent. However, splenic torsion could also occur in patients with abnormal laxity of the splenic peritoneal attachments. We report a case of a splenic torsion due to absence of splenic ligaments with pancreatic volvulus and partial involvement of descending colon in a 9-year-old boy.
