TI  - Power Doppler sonographic diagnosis of torsion in a wandering spleen.
AU  - Danaci, M
AU  - Belet, U
AU  - Yalin, T
AU  - Polat, V
AU  - Nurol, S
AU  - Selcuk, M B
PY  - 2000
T2  - Journal of clinical ultrasound : JCU
VL  - 28
IS  - 5
SP  - 246
EP  - 8
DO  - 10.1002/(sici)1097-0096(200006)28:5<246::aid-jcu6>3.0.co;2-#
AB  - A wandering spleen is a rare clinical entity resulting from congenital maldevelopment or acquired laxity of the spleen's suspensory ligaments; it may result in torsion of the spleen. We report the gray-scale sonographic, power Doppler sonographic, and CT findings in a case of wandering spleen torsion. The gray-scale sonograms showed a displaced spleen that appeared as a homogeneous, hypoechoic mass suggestive of an enlarged, ectopic spleen in the central abdomen. Power Doppler sonograms showed no blood flow in the parenchyma or hilum of the spleen and were consistent with torsion and infarction in the spleen. Contrast-enhanced CT scans showed a homogeneous, unenhanced mass that was diagnosed as a torsioned wandering spleen. The hilar vessels of the spleen were also unenhanced.
