AB  - To the Editor: I have operated on a woman with a very unusual form of bowel obstruction. Apparently, her bowel had been obstructed for about 10 days. She had a tender mass in the left upper quadrant, and was mildly anemic and mildly toxic at the time of surgery. There was torsion of a large spleen that measured about 10 by 12 by 14 cm. It had twisted five times on its pedicle, and two slips of small bowel were caught up in the edge of the pedicle. The bowel was viable, and therefore a simple splenectomy was done across. © 1980, Massachusetts Medical Society. All rights reserved.
