AB  - Background: Splenic torsion is a rare surgical emergency characterized by rotation of the spleen around its vascular pedicle, compromising blood flow and risking infarction. It often arises from a wandering spleen due to ligamentous laxity. Case presentation: A 19-year-old female with cerebral palsy presented with recurrent nausea, vomiting, and a palpable abdominal mass. Laboratory findings included leukocytosis (white blood cell count 18.5 & times;10 square/l) and elevated C-reactive protein (120mg/l). After 6 days of medical management, surgical consultation was requested. Contrast-enhanced abdominal computed tomography revealed an ectopically positioned spleen with the diagnostic whirl sign indicating pedicle torsion, heterogeneous nonenhancing parenchyma consistent with infarction, and moderate free intraperitoneal fluid suggesting hematoma. Surgical findings: Laparoscopic exploration showed an enlarged spleen displaced to the epigastrium and right upper quadrant, with an empty left subphrenic space and dense perisplenitis with adhesions to the colon and pancreas. Due to extensive adhesions, the procedure was converted to a midline laparotomy. Meticulous adhesiolysis preceded division of the splenocolic, lienocolic, and gastrosplenic ligaments, with ligation of short gastric vessels. Elevation of the spleen disclosed a pedicle twisted four times posteriorly. The pedicle was ligated and divided, enabling splenectomy. The peritoneal cavity underwent irrigation, followed by drain placement and layered closure. Outcome: The postoperative course was uneventful, with normalization of inflammatory markers by day 3 and discharge on day 5. Vaccination against encapsulated bacteria and lifelong antibiotic prophylaxis were advised. Conclusion: Splenic torsion merits suspicion in young patients with abdominal symptoms and a mass, especially with computed tomography whirl sign. Prompt surgical intervention is vital to prevent complications in at-risk individuals, such as those with cerebral palsy.
