TI  - The normal blood flow of intrasplenic arteries and veins in adult dogs and cats, shown by colour coded and pulsed wave Doppler ultrasonography
AU  - Nautrup, CP
AU  - Depken, U
AU  - von Götz, I
AU  - Günzel-Apel, AR
PY  - 2002
T2  - TIERARZTLICHE PRAXIS AUSGABE KLEINTIERE HEIMTIERE
VL  - 30
IS  - 4
SP  - 255
EP  - +
AB  - The intrasplenic arterial and venous blood flow was investigated by colour flow imaging and pulsed wave Doppler ultrasonography in 19 Beagles and 19 European short hair cats. All animals were not sedated, adult and had no cardiovascular disorders. The intrasplenic arteries and veins can be easily identified by colour flow imaging. Some experience is needed, when using pulsed wave Doppler ultrasonography in order to measure the blood flow within the small lienal vessels. The blood flow of the intrasplenic trabecular arteries shows typical rise and fall of all velocities. One cycle consists of an increase and decrease of the velocities and takes 40 to 60 seconds in the dog and 25 to 35 seconds in the cat. Therefore the variation in brightness of the colour coded vessels is more conspicuous in cats than in dogs. While there is no linear correlation between heart rate and cycle length in dogs, cats show a close linear correlation between the two parameters mentioned above. In addition, deep inspiration causes an immediate and short increase of velocity within the intrasplenic trabecular arteries only in dogs. The blood flow in the trabecular veins is very slow, close to the limit of the detection capacity of the present ultrasound systems. Only in two cats, an allusive increase and decrease of the velocities in the intrasplenic veins could be assumed. The phenomenon of periodical velocity changes in the canine and feline intrasplenic arteries leads to varying colour flow images and typical flow patterns. Factors such as respiration, heart rate, vessel size, and position of the Doppler gate have different influences on the blood flow velocities and the cycle length.
