TI  - A one hour rest-stress technetium-99m MIBI myocardial protocol
AU  - Clauss, R.P.
AU  - White, A.J.
AU  - Pilloy, W.
PY  - 1997
T2  - Physica Medica
VL  - 13
IS  - 3
SP  - 143
EP  - 146
AB  - A fast, effective image processing technique is described to reduce liver activity from Tc-99m MIBI cardiac perfusion SPECT images. Liver artefacts are thus removed from the reconstructed images and SPECT acquisitions can be done without the normal delay for liver clearance. The total imaging time is one hour. The method involves three SPECT acquisitions taken under identical conditions of orbit and patient position followed by planar image subtraction prior to SPECT reconstruction. The first acquisition is of the liver using Tc-99m colloid immediately followed by rest and stress cardiac perfusion acquisitions with Tc-99m MIBI. Since the colloid planar images contains liver uptake only while the MIBI planar images contain liver and myocardial uptake, appropriate normalisation and subtraction of the colloid images from the MIBI images results in MIBI planar images that contain normal myocardial uptake and minimal liver activity. SPECT reconstruction then gives myocardial tomographic images free from liver artefacts. However, since the stress acquisition is taken immediately after the rest acquisition, no significant radioactive decay will have occurred. The 'stress' acquisition will thus be the sum of true stress and rest data. The true stress data can then be obtained by planar image subtraction of the rest acquisition from the stress acquisition. Increased image noise that normally follows image subtraction was not significant in this procedure since high count statistics were present in the original images. This method has proven successful in eleven out of twelve patients so far investigated with results consistent with clinical data. The single failure being due to an upwardly displaced spleen adjacent to the heart in the colloid image. Subtraction of which from the MIBI images caused myocardial artefacts.
