AB  - 1) The spleen functions to remove senescent erythrocytes from the circulation and to make immune globulin and other proteins involved in the immune system. 2) Hereditary spherocytosis (HS) is an inherited hemolytic anemia caused by abnormalities in spectrin and other proteins which affect the structural integrity of the erythrocyte, which then become entrapped within the spleen leading to anemia and splenomegaly. 3) Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) is marked by thrombocytopenia due to antibody-mediated destruction of platelets primarily of splenic origin 4) Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) is a severe disease due to platelet aggregation in the microvasculature. TTP is marked by fever, thrombocytopenia, hemolytic anemia, neurological symptoms (e.g., headache, stroke), and renal insufficiency. 5) Wandering spleen is a condition in which the spleen may undergo torsion and requiring splenopexy. 6) Splenic cysts may be congenital, post-traumatic, parasitic, or infectious. Parasitic cysts due to Echinococcus may be treated with percutaneous infusions of normal saline or alcohol or splenectomy. Asymptomatic non-parasitic cysts <4 cm do not require treatment. 7) Splenectomy may be indicated for splenomegaly, anemia (hereditary spherocytosis), thrombocytopenia (idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura/thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura), splenic sequestration (sickle cell disease), pyruvate kinase deficiency, or symptomatic splenic cysts. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2022. All rights reserved.
