TI  - A case of torsion of a “wandering spleen” in a 29-year-old female with left-upper quadrant abdominal pain diagnosed on contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen
AU  - Cieslak, J.A.
AU  - Violari, E.G.
AU  - Yang, C.
PY  - 2018
T2  - Connecticut Medicine
VL  - 82
IS  - 9
SP  - 547
EP  - 549
AB  - Wandering spleen is a rare disorder in which there is laxity or congenital absence of the splenic suspensory ligaments, leading to inferior displacement and an ectopic location of the spleen within the abdomen and pelvis. As a result, a single attachment to the mesentery through the vascular pedicle at the splenic hilum predisposes the wandering spleen to axial rotation and torsion around its mesenteric root - causing ischemia and possible infarction. Wandering spleen may be asymptomatic, present as a painless mass, cause intermittent abdominal pain, or present as acute abdomen in the case of complete splenic infarction. Due to the variable and nonspecific nature of its presentation, radiological imaging is vital to its diagnosis. Once diagnosed, splenic torsion is treated with splenectomy (in cases where there is ischemia, splenomegaly, or infarction) or splenopexy if the spleen is otherwise normal. © 2018 Connecticut State Medical Society. All rights reserved.
