AB  - Splenic diseases represent important reasons for dogs to require emergency care. The majority of these cases are splenic masses, many of which are malignant in origin and result in spontaneous hemoperitoneum. Non-neoplastic splenic diseases, including torsion of the splenic pedicle and traumatic injury, may also result in acute illness in dogs necessitating urgent care. Cats are much less commonly affected by splenic disease in an emergent sense but important splenic diseases exist in this species nonetheless. This chapter will focus on the clinical presentation, approach, and complications in small animal emergency patients with splenic disease. © 2019 John Wiley and Sons, Inc.
