AB  - Objective To explore the clinical characteristics and treatments of accessory spleen torsion (AST) in children. Methods Clinical data were retrospectively reviewed for 3 AST children aged (137-163) month (one boy, two girls) from December 2019 to November 2021. Two cases were confirmed by intraoperative findings and postoperative pathology while another case was directly diagnosed by ultrasonography and computed tomography (CT). Literatures related to AST were retrieved from PubMed, WanFang and application document delivery from September 1962 to October 2021. Results Two cases of laparoscopic accessory splenectomy had a decent prognosis. In another child, volume of infarcled accessory spleen declined markedly after conservative measures. A total of 24 articles involving 24 cases related to AST in children were retrieved. The average onset age was (8. 87 ±5. 19) years, the proportion of patients with abdominal pain as a major symptom reached 87. 5%(21/24) and average long axis of infarcted accessory spleen was (6. 13 ± 2. 27) cm. Infarcted accessory spleens were located predominantly at greater omentum (9/24) and splenic hilum (4/24). Most cases were diagnosed by type B ultrasound and CT and only 22. 7%(5/22) cases could be diagnosed correctly pre-operation. The treatments for infarcted accessory spleen included conservative measures, laparotomy and laparoscopy. Conclusions AST is clinically rare in children and its diagnosis is made by ultrasonography and CT with a high misdiagnostic rate. Currently laparoscopy remains a major treatment. © 2023 Chinese Journal of Pediatric Surgery. All rights reserved.
