AB  - Introduction: Spleen infarction on wandering spleen is a rare cause of abdominal pain. From a review of the literature, the patterns of this pathology are described. Clinical observation: A woman of 50-years-old was admitted with acute abdominal pain. On CT examination, an unenhanced ovoid mass in umbilical area was found. Surgery confirmed the diagnosis of spleen torsion on wandering spleen. Discussion: Splenic torsion is a rare cause accounting for less than 0.2% of causes of abdominal pain. In absence of complications, the diagnosis of wandering spleen is difficult because of lack of symptom. The diagnosis of torsion can be affirmed in the presence of 3 signs: 1 – the absence of spleen in the upper left hypochondrium; 2 – the presence of an ovoid or comma-shaped abdominal mass, without or moderated enhancement after contrast injection; 3 – the presence of a wirl sign, corresponding to the torsion of the vascular pedicle of the spleen. Conclusion: Although rare, torsion of the spleen on wandering spleen is a cause of acute abdominal pain that is easily diagnosable in imaging. Radiologist should know its signs. © 2020 Société française de radiologie
