TI  - Torsion of wandering spleen in a patient with acute thrombocytopenia after splenopexy
AU  - Tsukayama, H.
AU  - Kittaka, H.
AU  - Nakayama, S.
PY  - 2019
T2  - Japanese Journal of Gastroenterological Surgery
VL  - 52
IS  - 7
SP  - 375
EP  - 382
DO  - 10.5833/jjgs.2018.0107
AB  - A 34-year-old woman was hospitalized due to severe left flank pain for two days. Contrast enhanced CT scan revealed torsion of a wandering spleen and splenic infarction and an emergency operation was performed. At laparotomy, the spleen was observed to be attached only at its hilum by the pedicle to the retroperitoneum and twisted in a 720-degree clockwise rotation around the pedicle. The spleen returned viable by reduction of the torsion about 8 hours after onset, therefore, we decided to perform splenopexy. However, a reoperation was performed one day after the first procedure because of postoperative hemorrhage caused by exacerbated thrombocytopenia. At the second surgery, bleeding was not from the spleen but from the abdominal wall. Several areas of patchy necrosis with marked splenomegaly were observed, suggesting that most of the spleen was not viable and thrombocytopenia might be caused by hypersplenism. Therefore, splenectomy was performed. The platelet count rose promptly, and the patient was discharged on POD 13. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first case describing that reperfusion of the spleen by reduction of torsion might complicate acute thrombocytopenia and hypersplenism, which required splenectomy. Contrary to the policy that preserving the organ takes priority whenever possible in management of torsion of a wandering spleen, the present case might be controversial. © 2019 The Japanese Society of Gastroenterological Surgery.
