EP  - 1071
DO  - 10.5833/jjgs.2015.0197
AB  - The patient was a 66-year-old woman with upper abdominal pain and nausea. Abdominal CT scan was performed to the gastric dilatation at the first medical examination. The conservative treatment by nasogastric tube could not improve these symptoms, so abdominal CT scan was performed again. We found spleen shifted to the posterior of the stomach, twisting antrum of stomach to pushing up the left upper abdomen. Gastrointestinal series showed antrum of stomach was located in a high position. Thus, we diagnosed gastric volvulus associated with wandering spleen. Repositioning by endoscope was tried, but untwisting of the gastric volvulus was difficult. So she was admitted to our department for the purpose of operation. Intoraoperative findings were mesenteroaxial gastric volvulus and perforation of posterior wall of stomach. We performed volvulus release, gastropexy and omentum covering. Postoperative course was uneventful and she was discharged on postoperative day 13. We highlight the possibility of gastric volvulus as a cause of acute abdominal pain with gastric dilatation. © 2016 The Japanese Society of Gastroenterological Surgery.
