AB  - The authors report a case of wandering spleen in a 10-year-old boy who was brought to the emergency service complaining of abdominal pain and constipation, that had begun several days earlier. On abdominal palpation, a hard, mobile mass was detected and was initially thought to be a fecaloma. However, abdominal radiograph and, particularly, ultrasound revealed a wandering spleen. Splenopexy was performed, but splenic infarction occurred and splenectomy was required. The imaging characteristics of this condition are shown and the diagnostic utility of ultrasound is stressed. We point out that wandering spleen should be taken into account in the differential diagnosis of abdominal masses in children. An exhaustive review of the literature is provided.
