AB  - In our case, a 19-year-old patient presented to the surgical clinic with recurrent abdominal pain and lower abdominal swelling, due to a skiing accident 21 days ago. Ultrasonography and magnetic resonance imaging of the abdomen demonstrated an enlarged spleen in the pelvis. Additional information was obtained by spiral computerised tomography scans. The hilum of the spleen showed a 720° twist, which resulted in venous congestion and splenomegaly. After laparotomy and detorsion of the spleen, its volume declined by approximately 30% within minutes. Splenopexy was performed with a vicryl-mesh. Follow-up examination after 3 months showed the spleen in anatomically correct position between stomach and left kidney. The patient had no discomfort.
