AB  - Wandering spleen is an uncommon clinical entity, which rarely affects children and adolescents. It is usually described in adults, especially women of childbearing age. It is caused by congenital absence of fixating ligaments of the spleen. The clinical presentation of wandering spleen is variable, but the most dangerous complication is splenic torsion. The case of an 11-year-old girl presented with acute abdominal pain and vomiting is reported. The preoperative diagnosis of acute torsion with infarction was made by the combination of ultrasonography and computed tomography. Urgent laparotomy was carried out and validated the presence the vascular pedicle torsion with enlarged congested spleen and severe infarction. Splenic ligaments were absent. Splenectomy was performed. Through a review of literature, we discuss the clinical presentations, diagnostic evaluation, treatment options, and clinical outcomes as they relate to wandering spleen. © 2009 Springer Verlag France.
