AB  - Torsion of accessory spleen is an extremely rare entity with variable clinical presentation. We report a case of torsion of an accessory spleen in a apiretic 37-year-old woman who presented to our emergency department with a continuous left abdominal pain, posteriorly radiated, without any gastrointestinal or urinary sign. Laboratory findings were normal. CT disclosed a hypodense mesenteric mass with peripheral fat tissue inflammation. After a few hours the patient presented with acute abdomen and was taken to the operating room with diagnosis of torsion of an accessory spleen. Preoperative diagnosis is difficult because of lack and non-specificity of presenting symptoms, until splenic torsion occurs: knowledge of this pathology and its radiological signs are fundamental for a correct diagnosis. © 2007 Elsevier Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.
