TI  - Spontaneous rupture of the pathological spleen in malaria
AU  - Davis, R.
PY  - 1973
T2  - South African Medical Journal
VL  - 47
IS  - 39
SP  - 1801
EP  - 1803
AB  - A case of spontaneous rupture of the spleen in acute malaria occurring in a Bantu female is presented. The pathogenesis and the various forms of spontaneous rupture of the spleen are discussed. Rupture may occur in the acute illness, or in a chronically enlarged spleen. The former is usually sudden and free, whereas the latter is usually of the 'delayed' type. The grossly enlarged spleen is liable to torsion or a laceration of its pedicle and should therefore be removed. When a ruptured pathologic spleen is found at operation, malaria should be excluded as an etiologic factor. Splenectomy in untreated malaria may result in an exacerbation of the clinical condition postoperatively.
